WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:11.940
Many curl scripts send credentials through authorization headers, API tokens, session values, client certificates, signed request data, or service-specific secret headers.

2
00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:18.840
Those values are sensitive even when the script is internal and even when the target service belongs to the same organization.

3
00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:22.240
Secrets can leak in more places than developers expect.

4
00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:34.020
They may appear in shell history, process listings, CI/CD logs, debug traces, environment dumps, support bundles, copied terminal output, or failure messages.

5
00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:40.040
A script that is safe during a happy path can still disclose a token during troubleshooting.

6
00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:42.880
Avoid hard-coded credentials in scripts.

7
00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:48.500
Use approved secret-management or runtime-injection mechanisms where practical.

8
00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:56.920
Keep the secret scoped to the job that needs it, and avoid passing sensitive values to child processes, helper tools, or logs that do not need them.

9
00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:00.000
Headers deserve the same care as payloads.

10
00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:08.180
An authorization header, session header, tenant identifier, or signed request value may reveal access or internal structure.

11
00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:13.640
Logging should show enough context to debug the request without printing secrets.

12
00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:16.860
Redaction should be deliberate, not an afterthought.

13
00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:20.100
Debugging should be scoped and temporary.

14
00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:27.960
Verbose request output can be helpful, but it may include headers, response bodies, or connection details that should not be stored broadly.

15
00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:34.940
Debug settings should be turned on only when needed and reviewed before being used in shared CI/CD logs.

16
00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:38.420
Secret handling is part of the curl design.

17
00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:45.880
The script should define how secrets enter, where they are used, how they are redacted, and what evidence remains after the request.

18
00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:50.460
That discipline reduces accidental disclosure while preserving operational visibility.
